{% comment %}
  Appx — Product Specs Table
  Resolves the current product to its assigned template HANDLE via the shop-level
  routing map (feature 43, context/features/43-storefront-routing-resolution.md).

  Params (render scope is isolated — both passed explicitly):
  - product: the current product
  - routing: the parsed `shop.metafields["$app"].routing.value` json object, i.e.
             { defaultTemplateHandle, byType, byVendor, byCollection, byTag,
               byProduct, excludedProductGids } (feature 40 shape, written by 41).

  Emits: the resolved template handle STRING, or nothing. Liquid render scope is
  isolated (a snippet cannot return a value), so the block CAPTURES this output.

  Resolution order (data-model §9, feature 45 Decision B). The ACTIVE rule set is
  disjoint (feature 42), so at most one key matches — the order is efficiency, never
  precedence. An EXPLICIT single-product assignment (`byProduct`) is checked BEFORE
  the exclude gate, so an excluded product still reaches its own dedicated table
  (the "all products EXCEPT X, and X gets its own table" story); the exclude gate
  then only carves products out of the BROAD tiers:
    byProduct[<product GID>]
      -> excluded? -> nothing
      -> byType[type] -> byVendor[vendor]
        -> byCollection[<collection GID>] (scan) -> defaultTemplateHandle
  This map is now the ONLY source of an assignment. The per-product override
  metafield that used to sit ahead of it in the block was removed 2026-08-04
  (see `blocks/spec_table.liquid`), so `byProduct` is the whole of the
  explicit-single-product story.

  Keys are GID-faithful (feature 40): byProduct / byCollection keys are the raw
  `gid://shopify/...` token, but Liquid only exposes the numeric `.id`, so we
  CONSTRUCT the GID token before each lookup. byType / byVendor keys are the raw
  selector strings (a blank product type/vendor indexes a key that never exists,
  so it simply falls through). TAG routing is intentionally NOT read — `byTag` is
  always {} (post-MVP; absent from the AssignmentScope enum).

  Collections are scanned in 50-item chunks (Shopify caps `for` at 50), breaking
  on the first hit — mirroring the block's rows loop.
{% endcomment %}
{%- liquid
  assign handle = ''

  if routing != blank
    assign pgid = 'gid://shopify/Product/' | append: product.id

    # An explicit single-product assignment wins even for an excluded product
    # (feature 45 Decision B) — it is checked BEFORE the exclude gate.
    assign handle = routing.byProduct[pgid]

    # The broad tiers are carved out by excludedProductGids: an excluded product
    # matches none of them (renders nothing unless byProduct claimed it above).
    if handle == blank
      unless routing.excludedProductGids contains pgid
        assign handle = routing.byType[product.type]

        if handle == blank
          assign handle = routing.byVendor[product.vendor]
        endif

        if handle == blank
          assign coll_chunks = product.collections.size | plus: 49 | divided_by: 50
          for chunk in (1..coll_chunks)
            assign coll_offset = chunk | minus: 1 | times: 50
            for collection in product.collections offset: coll_offset limit: 50
              assign cgid = 'gid://shopify/Collection/' | append: collection.id
              assign coll_hit = routing.byCollection[cgid]
              if coll_hit != blank
                assign handle = coll_hit
                break
              endif
            endfor
            if handle != blank
              break
            endif
          endfor
        endif

        if handle == blank
          assign handle = routing.defaultTemplateHandle
        endif
      endunless
    endif
  endif

  echo handle
-%}
